WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.254
Timeouts are essential because child processes do not always finish
quickly or predictably.

2
00:00:05.254 --> 00:00:11.837
A process may hang, wait for input, slow down under load, produce too much
output, or depend on an unavailable service.

3
00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:17.357
Without a timeout, a web request, worker slot, or scheduled job can remain
occupied indefinitely.

4
00:00:17.357 --> 00:00:22.611
Timeouts turn unbounded execution into an operational decision the
application can handle.

5
00:00:22.611 --> 00:00:25.271
Output management also affects availability.

6
00:00:25.271 --> 00:00:36.310
If a process produces more output than expected, captures are too large,
or streams are not handled correctly, the parent process can consume
memory, block, or lose useful diagnostic information.

7
00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:42.628
The application should decide whether output is captured, streamed,
limited, stored, redacted, or discarded.

8
00:00:42.628 --> 00:00:48.147
Standard output and standard error should be treated as potentially
sensitive until classified.

9
00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:52.869
Exit codes and check behavior should be translated into safe application
outcomes.

10
00:00:52.869 --> 00:01:00.650
Some failures are expected business problems, some are dependency
failures, some are timeouts, and some are unexpected internal errors.

11
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:08.763
User-facing responses should avoid raw command strings, sensitive
arguments, full internal output, and unnecessary implementation detail.

12
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:15.413
Internal logs can preserve safe diagnostic context, correlation IDs,
operation names, durations, and reason codes.

13
00:01:15.413 --> 00:01:21.864
Resource controls and retry behavior prevent subprocess failures from
becoming denial-of-service conditions.

14
00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:29.445
Concurrency limits, queue limits, output limits, cancellation behavior,
and process termination rules should be part of the design.

15
00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:38.755
Retrying a failed child process may be appropriate for transient failures,
but repeated execution can duplicate work, amplify outages, or leave
partial artifacts.

16
00:01:38.755 --> 00:01:42.280
Failure should be predictable, observable, and recoverable.
